{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_strip_regularity","record":{"id":"scalar.source_strip_regularity","kind":"claim","label":"Concrete strip regularity for every admissible coefficient","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-13-source-regularity","locator":"Control/Regularity.lean:22–42; sourceStripRegularity","statement":"For every W:BrownianSpace and gamma:OrderParameter, SourceStripRegularity W gamma holds: for each T in Ico 0 1 there exist Kv,Lv,K,L,D,Lu,La supplying ValueStrip, GradientStrip, nonnegative D,Lu,La, a uniform curvature bound, and the stated joint time-space Lipschitz bounds for gradient and curvature on Icc 0 T × R.","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-13-cite-strip-regularity"],"boundary":"No minimizer or diffusion premise. The constants are chosen separately for each fixed strip; no uniform terminal bound is asserted. Definition: Control/StripRegularity.lean:28–37. Authored correspondence to inspected frozen source; unverified status is retained. No fresh Lean build, elaborated dependency extraction, axiom audit, Comparator acceptance, or independent mathematical acceptance is claimed."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_strip_regularity","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/formal-contact","anchor":"formal-contact","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/formal-contact","anchor":"formal-contact","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"formal-scalar-contact\"></a>\n\n### 10.1 Contact from convexity on constant intervals\n\nThere is a second way to obtain $q=t$ from the variational inequalities.\nIt uses convexity on intervals where the tail potential is positive,\nrather than first proving full Stieltjes support. This is the argument\nused by the scalar implementation; it does not depend on the formal\nfull-support theorem.\n\nIn the scalar model put $q(t)=\\mathbb E[u(t,X_t)^2]$,\n$f(t)=q(t)-t$ and $G(t)=\\int_t^1f(s)\\,ds$. The source first obtains\n$G\\ge0$, $G(1)=0$, $q(0)=0$ and $G'=-f$. Compact-mass variations\nalso show that on any interval where $G>0$ the coefficient is constant:\nif its cumulative mass increased between two points there, integration\nof the strictly positive $G$ against that positive increment would\ncontradict the mass-removal variational inequality. This is the content\nof the [scalar contact-integral argument][scalar-contact-integral].\n\nSuppose now that $G(x)>0$ for some $0<x<1$. Choose the component\n$(a,b)$ of this positivity set containing $x$, allowing $a=0$ or\n$b=1$. Its coefficient is constant, say $c\\ge0$, and $G(b)=0$.\nAt the left endpoint, $f(a)=0$: this is the contact derivative when\n$a>0$, and $q(0)=0$ when $a=0$.\n\nIf $c>0$, one also has $G(a)=0$. The only extra case is $a=0$:\nright continuity gives $\\gamma(0)=c>0$, so the mass-removal condition\nat that initial atom forces $G(0)=0$. The mean-value theorem on\n$[a,x]$ and $[x,b]$ now provides points $r<s$ with\n$f(r)<0<f(s)$. There is a later point $z\\le b$, with $z<1$, for\nwhich $f(z)\\le f(s)$. For $b<1$ take $z=b$, since contact gives\n$f(b)=0$. For $b=1$, choose $z$ sufficiently close to one and use\n$f(z)=q(z)-z\\le1-z<f(s)$.\n\nThe three intervals $[a,r]$, $[r,s]$ and $[s,z]$ therefore have\nsecant slopes for $f$ of signs respectively negative, positive and\nnonpositive. Applying the mean-value theorem once more gives three\nordered points where $f'$ has those signs. This is impossible when\n$f'=q'-1$ is convex: its middle value cannot exceed the larger of\nits two exterior values. The scalar source supplies precisely this\nnonstrict convexity on a constant interval. See\n[the positive-hump argument and its endpoint condition][scalar-hump]\nand [the curvature-moment convexity lemma][scalar-convexity].\n\nIf $c=0$, the source instead gives convexity of $f=q-t$. A mean-value\nargument between $x$ and $b$ gives $f(s)>0$; the same right-endpoint\nargument produces $z>s$ with $f(z)\\le f(s)$. Since $f(a)=0$,\n$$\n\\frac{f(s)-f(a)}{s-a}>0\\ge\n\\frac{f(z)-f(s)}{z-s},\n$$\ncontradicting monotonicity of adjacent secant slopes of a convex\nfunction. This case does not need $G(a)=0$; see\n[the zero-coefficient hump lemma][scalar-zero-hump].\n\nThus $G$ cannot have a positive component, and $G\\equiv0$ on\n$(0,1)$. Differentiation gives $q(t)=t$ there, with the origin supplied\nseparately. The [scalar assembly][scalar-contact-assembly] then obtains\nthe unit curvature moment, finite Gaussian approximation and terminal\nvalue identities from this contact equality. Nonstrict convexity suffices\nfor this scalar route. The strict curvature estimate in Sections 4–5\nhas the further job of excluding gaps in Stieltjes support. These are\nsource-level proof reconstructions, not a fresh Lean acceptance or a\nclaim that the two formal models have been composed.\n\n","span":{"start_byte":166665,"end_byte":169976,"start_line":3555,"end_line":3620},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=formal-contact","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=formal-contact"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_strip_regularity&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_strip_regularity&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_strip_regularity&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_strip_regularity&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_fixed_strip_application%22%2C%22scalar.source_strip_regularity%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","to":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.sourc